{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>הבה.</b> 'הבה את אשתי' (Genesis 29:21) is masculine language, and this is correct, like 'לכה נא עמנו.' For the feminine, 'הבי המטפחת' (Ruth 3:15), 'לכי ובאי' (1 Kings 1:13). Because they speak these words frequently, they say 'הבה נרדה' (Genesis 11), and for the feminine 'הבה נא אבוא אליך' (ibid. 38:16), like 'לכה נשקה את אבינו יין' (ibid. 19:32):" "נתחכמה. Let us seek a wise course so that he not multiply:" "לו. On his account, like 'אמרי לי' (ibid. 20:13). Know that all verbs, whether past or future, have the verbal noun within their force — I mean the noun derived from the verb, which is defective, is understood from the force of the verbs — like 'והנה ברכת ברך' (Numbers 23:11), 'וברך ולא אשיבנה' (ibid. 23:20), and the meaning is 'and he blessed a blessing,' and likewise 'וברוב יועצים תקום' (Proverbs 15:22), and the complete sense is 'many counselors give counsel and it stands,' and likewise 'כי תקראנה קורות מלחמה' — says R. Marinus — for the meaning is 'ועלה מן הארץ,' like 'ועלינו.' And the matter is thus so that the Satan not cause his mouth to stumble, but in my opinion there is no need."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The phrase 'כי תקראנה קורות מלחמה' has no biblical source cited and may be a quotation or paraphrase whose origin is uncertain; translated hyper-literally. The referent of 'א\"ר מרינוס' is unclear; resolved as 'says R. Marinus' by vocalization inference."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:46.197579453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}